1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,697
A spiral staircase.

2
00:00:01,697 --> 00:00:03,043
A coiled phone cord.

3
00:00:03,043 --> 00:00:04,163
A ball of wool.

4
00:00:04,563 --> 00:00:07,787
Each one is a long line,
folded into a shape.

5
00:00:08,236 --> 00:00:10,507
Diya was untangling her earphone wire.

6
00:00:10,507 --> 00:00:13,862
It had curled into a spring,
then knotted into a ball.

7
00:00:13,862 --> 00:00:16,467
She asked: does a protein fold like this?

8
00:00:16,467 --> 00:00:18,421
And why would its shape matter?

9
00:00:18,771 --> 00:00:20,480
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:21,380
Yes.

11
00:00:20,964 --> 00:00:23,357
A protein chain folds in four levels.

12
00:00:23,357 --> 00:00:26,975
And without the right fold,
a protein cannot do its job.

13
00:00:27,475 --> 00:00:32,286
By the end, you will know the primary,
secondary, tertiary and quaternary

14
00:00:32,286 --> 00:00:35,201
structure, with
haemoglobin as our example.

15
00:00:35,851 --> 00:00:37,998
First, what does structure even mean?

16
00:00:38,348 --> 00:00:40,140
It depends on who you ask.

17
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:43,761
A chemist writes a formula,
like the one for common salt.

18
00:00:43,761 --> 00:00:47,130
An organic chemist draws a
flat picture, like benzene.

19
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:49,203
A physicist imagines a 3D shape.

20
00:00:49,653 --> 00:00:52,219
Biologists describe a
protein at four levels.

21
00:00:52,219 --> 00:00:54,618
Each level adds one more layer of shape.

22
00:00:55,268 --> 00:00:57,426
Let's zoom into a famous protein.

23
00:00:57,776 --> 00:01:00,485
Inside your blood are red blood cells.

24
00:01:00,485 --> 00:01:01,887
They carry oxygen.

25
00:01:02,387 --> 00:01:03,302
Zoom closer.

26
00:01:03,302 --> 00:01:06,209
Each red blood cell is
packed with haemoglobin.

27
00:01:06,209 --> 00:01:09,634
Adult human haemoglobin has
four parts, called subunits.

28
00:01:10,134 --> 00:01:11,735
Zoom to one subunit.

29
00:01:11,735 --> 00:01:17,001
It is one long amino acid chain, coiled
in places and folded into a ball.

30
00:01:17,651 --> 00:01:20,309
Now build a protein's
shape, level by level.

31
00:01:20,659 --> 00:01:21,559
Level one.

32
00:01:21,548 --> 00:01:24,912
The order of amino acids
is the primary structure.

33
00:01:24,912 --> 00:01:27,806
The first one is the
N-terminal amino acid.

34
00:01:27,806 --> 00:01:30,648
The last one is the C-terminal amino acid.

35
00:01:31,398 --> 00:01:32,298
Level two.

36
00:01:32,245 --> 00:01:36,606
Some parts of the chain coil into
a helix, like a spiral staircase.

37
00:01:36,606 --> 00:01:38,699
This is the secondary structure.

38
00:01:39,449 --> 00:01:40,366
Level three.

39
00:01:40,366 --> 00:01:44,074
The whole chain folds on itself
like a hollow ball of wool.

40
00:01:44,074 --> 00:01:46,892
This is the tertiary
structure, a 3D shape.

41
00:01:47,642 --> 00:01:48,542
Level four.

42
00:01:48,392 --> 00:01:50,864
Some proteins have more
than one folded chain.

43
00:01:50,864 --> 00:01:53,955
How these subunits sit together
is the quaternary structure.

44
00:01:54,705 --> 00:01:56,338
And remember why it matters.

45
00:01:56,338 --> 00:02:00,408
The tertiary structure is absolutely
necessary for many activities of proteins.

46
00:02:01,158 --> 00:02:02,146
Remember this.

47
00:02:02,146 --> 00:02:03,983
Order, coil, ball, team.

48
00:02:04,583 --> 00:02:07,923
Now read a primary
structure, like a sentence.

49
00:02:08,273 --> 00:02:10,622
We imagine the protein as a line.

50
00:02:10,622 --> 00:02:14,217
The left end is the first
amino acid, the N-terminal.

51
00:02:14,217 --> 00:02:17,498
We read across to the
right end, the C-terminal.

52
00:02:17,948 --> 00:02:22,929
Change the order, and you get a different
protein, even with the same beads.

53
00:02:22,929 --> 00:02:27,489
The primary structure is positional
information: which amino acid is first,

54
00:02:27,489 --> 00:02:29,505
which is second, and so on.

55
00:02:30,155 --> 00:02:32,235
Two levels are often confused.

56
00:02:32,235 --> 00:02:34,426
Let's compare them side by side.

57
00:02:34,776 --> 00:02:39,264
Tertiary structure is about one
chain folding on itself into a ball.

58
00:02:39,714 --> 00:02:43,674
Quaternary structure is about many
folded chains fitting together.

59
00:02:43,674 --> 00:02:47,093
Haemoglobin, with its four
subunits, is the example.

60
00:02:47,743 --> 00:02:49,679
Let's look closer at the helix.

61
00:02:50,029 --> 00:02:52,776
Only some portions of
the chain form a helix.

62
00:02:52,776 --> 00:02:54,953
Other regions fold into other forms.

63
00:02:55,403 --> 00:02:57,954
And proteins show only
right-handed helices.

64
00:02:57,954 --> 00:03:01,133
Picture a spiral staircase
you climb turning one way.

65
00:03:01,733 --> 00:03:02,633
Your turn.

66
00:03:02,470 --> 00:03:04,155
Haemoglobin has four subunits.

67
00:03:04,155 --> 00:03:07,966
Which level describes how they fit
together: tertiary, or quaternary?

68
00:03:07,966 --> 00:03:09,050
Pause and decide.

69
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:13,150
Quaternary!

70
00:03:12,964 --> 00:03:14,665
Tertiary is one chain folding.

71
00:03:14,665 --> 00:03:17,047
Quaternary is how the
subunits are arranged.

72
00:03:17,697 --> 00:03:19,207
Now, a worked example.

73
00:03:19,207 --> 00:03:21,458
Name the level shown in each picture.

74
00:03:21,808 --> 00:03:25,267
Picture one: beads in a
row, each with a number.

75
00:03:25,267 --> 00:03:26,911
Only the order matters.

76
00:03:26,911 --> 00:03:28,967
That is the primary structure.

77
00:03:29,417 --> 00:03:32,681
Picture two: a stretch of the
chain coiled like a spring.

78
00:03:32,681 --> 00:03:34,499
That is the secondary structure.

79
00:03:34,949 --> 00:03:38,046
Picture three: four folded
balls fitting together.

80
00:03:38,046 --> 00:03:40,129
That is the quaternary structure.

81
00:03:40,779 --> 00:03:42,241
Now, the exam trap.

82
00:03:42,591 --> 00:03:45,785
Many students think the four subunits
of haemoglobin are all the same.

83
00:03:46,185 --> 00:03:49,446
But haemoglobin has two alpha
subunits and two beta subunits.

84
00:03:49,446 --> 00:03:53,494
Only the two alphas match each other,
and the two betas match each other.

85
00:03:54,144 --> 00:03:55,414
Where is this asked?

86
00:03:55,414 --> 00:03:59,269
As match questions on the four levels,
and statement questions on helices

87
00:03:59,269 --> 00:04:00,267
and haemoglobin.

88
00:04:00,867 --> 00:04:02,041
Exam level now.

89
00:04:02,041 --> 00:04:04,537
Match each level with its description.

90
00:04:04,537 --> 00:04:05,761
Pause and solve.

91
00:04:11,161 --> 00:04:12,597
The answer is A.

92
00:04:12,597 --> 00:04:14,923
One-r, two-p, three-s, four-q.

93
00:04:15,423 --> 00:04:17,874
Primary is the order of amino acids.

94
00:04:17,874 --> 00:04:19,502
Secondary is the helix.

95
00:04:20,002 --> 00:04:21,630
Tertiary is the folded ball.

96
00:04:21,630 --> 00:04:23,809
Quaternary is how subunits are arranged.

97
00:04:24,409 --> 00:04:25,949
Here is your recap.

98
00:04:25,949 --> 00:04:27,532
Screenshot this card.

99
00:04:30,732 --> 00:04:32,918
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

100
00:04:33,318 --> 00:04:34,218
One.

101
00:04:33,838 --> 00:04:36,144
What is the N-terminal amino acid?

102
00:04:37,044 --> 00:04:37,944
Two.

103
00:04:37,403 --> 00:04:39,359
Which type of helix is found in proteins?

104
00:04:40,259 --> 00:04:41,159
Three.

105
00:04:40,772 --> 00:04:43,226
Name the subunits of
adult human haemoglobin.

106
00:04:44,326 --> 00:04:48,759
Next time: a tiny pocket in a folded
protein can speed up a reaction ten

107
00:04:48,759 --> 00:04:49,779
million times.

108
00:04:49,779 --> 00:04:50,679
How?

109
00:04:50,265 --> 00:04:52,868
We find out in enzymes, how they work.
